 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Family",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "A1101, A1102, A1103, A1104 and A1106 are unipolar Hall-effect switches made in BiCMOS, successors to the Allegro 312x and 314x unipolar switches. Each chip holds a voltage regulator, the Hall element, a small-signal amplifier, a Schmitt trigger and an NMOS output transistor. The five devices are the same apart from their magnetic switch points. They are AEC-Q100 qualified."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Supply and protection",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The on-chip regulator lets the part run from 3.8 V to 24 V and stays stable with no bypass capacitor. Protection on the die gives a ±30 V absolute rating on VCC, including reverse battery, with no external parts. VCC is clamped by an internal zener of 32 V minimum at 10.5 mA (25 °C); the zener current is limited to ICC(max) + 3 mA. The output withstands 30 V off and –0.5 V reverse and sinks up to 25 mA."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Trimming",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The switch points are programmed at the end of the line, after packaging, which removes offsets that package stress introduces. The Hall element shape and low-offset amplifiers reduce noise and the offset left by overmolding, temperature change and thermal stress."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Switching",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "A south-pole field perpendicular to the Hall element turns the output on (low) once it rises above the operate point BOP; the output then sinks up to 25 mA at VOUT(SAT). When the field drops below the release point BRP the output turns off and goes high. BOP – BRP is the hysteresis BHYS, which keeps the output switching cleanly through mechanical vibration and electrical noise. A north field, or no field, leaves the output off."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Power-on state",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "If the part powers up with the field between BRP and BOP, the output state is undefined; it becomes correct the first time the field goes above BOP or below BRP. With the field outside that band by 5 G and VCC rising slower than 2.5 V/µs, the output is valid within tPO = 4 µs maximum."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Continuous-time operation",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "These are continuous-time switches: no chopper stabilization, so the power-on settling and frequency response are the fastest available, and the offset that packaging leaves is removed by the after-packaging trim instead. Chopper-stabilized switches cancel offset on the chip without trimming but settle more slowly and respond to lower frequencies. A continuous-time part suits battery-powered systems that pulse VCC at a low duty cycle to save power, since the short tPO sets how short each pulse can be."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Duty-cycled supply",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "In a pulsed-supply system (field below BRP, output expected high) the sequence is: VCC is applied; the output settles to its correct state before tPO(max); after tPO(max) the output is valid; the controller samples the output; VCC is removed."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Power derating",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "The junction must stay at or below TJ(max) = 165 °C. RθJA lumps every heat path from die to ambient air and depends mostly on the board's effective thermal conductivity, including nearby parts and traces; the die-to-case path is a small part of it, and ambient temperature and air flow matter. Example at TA = 25 °C, VCC = 12 V, ICC = 4 mA, RθJA = 140 °C/W: PD = 48 mW, ΔT = 7 °C, TJ = 32 °C. Example at TA = 150 °C, package UA, worst case RθJA = 165 °C/W, ICC(max) = 7.5 mA: ΔTmax = 165 – 150 = 15 °C, PD(max) = 15 °C ÷ 165 °C/W = 91 mW, VCC(est) = 91 mW ÷ 7.5 mA = 12.1 V. The device is reliable up to VCC(est); if VCC(est) is below VCC(max) = 24 V, running between VCC(est) and VCC(max) needs a lower RθJA, and if VCC(est) is above VCC(max) the whole supply range is reliable."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Packages",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "LH is a flat 3-pin SOT23W for surface mount; UA is a small 3-pin through-hole SIP, in a matrix HD style and a chopper style, the chopper style not for new design. All have 100% matte-tin-plated, lead-free leadframes. LH parts are marked NNT or NNN (N: last three digits of the part number, T: temperature code letter); UA parts carry the supplier emblem with NNN (last three digits) or NNT (last two digits and temperature code)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Hall element position",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "LH: the Hall element centre is 1.49 mm from the body end on the pin 2 side and 0.96 mm from the body edge on the pin 3 side, 0.28 ±0.04 mm below the branded face. UA: the element centre is 2.04 mm (nominal) from the body side and 1.44 mm (nominal) from the top of the body, 0.50 ±0.08 mm below the branded face on the matrix style and 0.50 mm (reference) on the chopper style."
  }
 ],
